 have\n\n$$\nP(X)={∅,{m},{n},{m,n}}.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186054987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186051422 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186054987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371651 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186054987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186054987 1071 "If $C = {{2}, 3}$, then $P(C) =$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186054987 1071 "If $C={{2},3},$ then $P(C)=$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186054987 1074 "The power set of $C,$ denoted $P(C),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $C,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nSo, let's list all possible subsets of $C$ using a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/2f/2f7eed3dea71ea1492e2c9762c39b4292c9fd275b3c6a18832b04454f78dc114.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram, the subsets of $C$ are\n\n$$\n∅={},\\,{{2}},\\,{3},\\,{{2},3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(X)={∅,{{2}},{3},{{2},3}}.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186059818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371519 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186059819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371512 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186059820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371526 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186059820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371540 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186059820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373277 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186060410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059052 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186060410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373250 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186060410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186060410 1071 "Consider the set $X = {a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h}$. Which subset must be placed into the blank in the set below so that it represents a partition of $X$?\n\n${{a, b}, {d, e}, {f, h}, ____}$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186060410 1071 "Consider the set $X={a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h}.$ Which subset must be placed into the blank in the set below so that it represents a partition of $X?$\n\n$$\n{{a,b},{d,e},{f,h},\\,____\\,}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186060410 1074 "A partition of a set $X$ is a collection of non-empty subsets of $X$ where every element of $X$ is contained in *exactly* one subset. \n\nTo find the subset that goes in the blank, we must identify which elements of $X$ are not yet contained within any of the given subsets.\n\nLet's go through each element of $X$ and check:\n\n- $a\\in {a,b}\\,✓$\n\n- $b\\in {a,b}\\,✓$\n\n- $c$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\n- $d\\in {d,e}\\,✓$\n\n- $e\\in {d,e}\\,✓$\n\n- $f\\in {f,h}\\,✓$\n\n- $g$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\n- $h\\in {f,h}\\,✓$\n\nThe elements $c$ and $g$ are not yet contained within any of the given subsets. Therefore, the subset that must go in the blank is ${c,g}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064022 1071 "Each side of a square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}$. How fast is the area of the square decreasing when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}$?\n\n![](images/a0/a06eb0963dc6d01e45acb9349e5232ba70a11c98c99ee63c2ec0dae3d748f5ed.png)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186064022 1071 "Each side of a square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the square decreasing when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}?$\n\n![](images/a0/a06eb0963dc6d01e45acb9349e5232ba70a11c98c99ee63c2ec0dae3d748f5ed.png)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064022 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -2\n$$\n\nAlso, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}$, we have $x=10$. Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= (-2) \\cdot 2(10) =-40cm^{2}/min\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square decreases at a rate of $40\\,\\text{cm}^2/\\text{min}$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186064022 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} .\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min},$ so we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -2.$ Also, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(-2)\\cdot 2(10)=-40{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square decreases at a rate of $40\\,\\text{cm}^2/\\text{min}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064023 1071 "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $20 \\text{ cm}^2\\text{/s}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064023 1071 "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $20 \\text{cm}^2\\text{/s}$. If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186064023 1074 "This situation is different. Here, the *area* is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\text{ cm[math]^2[/math]/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064023 1074 "This situation is different. Here, the *area* is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= -20cm[math]^2[/math]/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-20 &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot 2x \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= -\\frac{10}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186064024 1071 "![](images/26/2677121139896da73eca5277c7c23f193467001eabba976cb48b2232f4bcbad6.png)\n\nIn a testing facility, a scientist is increasing the diagonals of a simple square at a rate of $12 \\text{ mm}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate, in squared millimeters per minute, is the area of the square increasing when the length of the diagonal is $6\\, \\text{mm}?$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064024 1071 "![](images/26/2677121139896da73eca5277c7c23f193467001eabba976cb48b2232f4bcbad6.png)\n\nIn a testing facility, a scientist is increasing the diagonals of a simple square at a rate of $12 \\text{mm}\\text{/min}$. At what rate, in squared millimeters per minute, is the area of the square increasing when the length of the diagonal is $6\\, \\text{mm}$?" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186064024 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{h^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dh}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dh}(\\frac{h^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dh}{dt} \\cdot h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min}$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dh}{dt} &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6$. Substituting this information into our expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= 12 \\cdot 6 = 72mm^{2}/min\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186064024 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =12\\cdot 6=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064025 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(A) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{h^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dh}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dh}(\\frac{h^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{dh}{dt} \\cdot h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\n, so replacing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}\n$$\n\nin the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\frac{dh}{dt} &= h\\frac{dh}{dt} \\\\\n2\\frac{dh}{dt} &= h\\frac{dh}{dt} \\\\\n2 &= h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2$, and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186064025 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}$, so replacing $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 2 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372471 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372896 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372899 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372902 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371596 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372905 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373069 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373076 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373194 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064380 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371610 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064380 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371617 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064380 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372911 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064380 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372914 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371603 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372546 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186064381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372908 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186068710 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373159 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186068864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372990 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186083260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371657 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186083261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373003 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186083847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186081983 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186083847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373083 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186083847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186083847 1071 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨8{t}^{2},\\,45⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=3.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186083847 1071 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n$r = ⟨8t^{2}, 45⟩, t > 0$.\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t = 3$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186083847 1074 "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(45)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16t,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨16(3),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨48,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186087546 1071 "Write the complex number ![](images/af/af471b7c6676fbc559c8ad2df178f0837e6a56d05b2f50580ebccc25b623f3c3.png), shown in the following Argand diagram, in exponential form.\n\n![](images/33/335311049419928acc946721242c6e8e4096313dca1290b89b1cc7a2c30748fd.png)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186087546 1071 "Write the complex number ![](images/c9/c93c5bf19eefeb4da980b81d46c4df10b3ff10aca21937aed885ef4111b1c9a7.png), shown in the following Argand diagram, in exponential form.\n\n![](images/33/335311049419928acc946721242c6e8e4096313dca1290b89b1cc7a2c30748fd.png)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186087546 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n![](images/5d/5d6456ae3c5f168a14e761b44085defd76cd2292e8fa8d9b0eedf2cb55d97563.png)\n\nTo write it in the form ![](images/0e/0eb9bfc5066fce3fe0434f4dedf05cea2fff1e2fe38b32d4223eaf177ca2b654.png) where ![](images/d3/d3e8106a9b65dceed9a9c4e6fbc6341c0268517854b72f6e34fc3ec80117b915.png) and ![](images/60/6017ce5c562b2c1a2d1a47371c54820b04852fb851056aa171f88c411d92bd45.png) we first calculate ![](images/e3/e3862c77b505ea48e95acf4d632fa692c866c65079a2ac6b17543fd54e1c20ca.png)\n![](images/d