third term is $\\frac{1}{36}.$ What is the sum of the first $6$ terms?" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186092700 1074 "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio between the consecutive terms ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{36})}{(\\frac{1}{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of ${a}_{1}.$ To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{12} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4},N=6$ and $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6})}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{6}})}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}\\cdot (\\frac{{3}^{6}-1}{{3}^{6}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot (\\frac{728}{729}) \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{243}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186092713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090448 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186092713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373057 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186092713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186092713 1071 "Find the **polar** coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r = 4$ intersects the negative $x$-axis." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186092713 1071 "Find the **polar** coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=4$ intersects the negative $x$-axis." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186092713 1074 "The polar curve $r=4$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $x$-axis at the point where $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\pi .$\n\n![](images/1e/1e77c7521d2bfd617773666ee6e8cab06442e347382b230f58b00af26a085645.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(4,\\pi ).$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186092906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091606 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186092906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373394 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186092906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186092906 1071 "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186092906 1071 "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186092906 1074 "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|7\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 5| \\\\  & =|35-5| \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186100899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371858 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186100900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373009 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186105728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372987 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186115398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373124 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186119860 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371505 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186119866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371727 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186119866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373229 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186119866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373236 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186119887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371484 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124468 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373131 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371755 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373012 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373145 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124481 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371741 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124481 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371748 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124481 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373138 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124572 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372355 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124574 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373243 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372208 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372327 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373331 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186124680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372697 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186125061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122936 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186125061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373019 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186125061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186125061 1071 "If $f(x, y) = \\cos x + y$, use the differential $df$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x, y)$ from $(4π, 8)$ to $(13, 8.5)$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186125061 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(4\\pi ,8)$ to $(13,8.5).$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186125061 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(4\\pi ,8),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(4\\pi ,8) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi \\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(4\\pi +Δx,8+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(4\\pi ,8)$ to $(13,8.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =13-4\\pi , \\\\ Δy & =8.5-8=0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (13-4\\pi )+1\\cdot (0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186128842 1071 "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\nx = {y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{y^2}3, \\qquad y=\\dfrac2x, \\qquad y=\\dfrac4x.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186128842 1071 "Using the change of variables\n\n$u = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy$,\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n$x = {y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{y^2}3, \\qquad y=\\dfrac2x, \\qquad y=\\dfrac4x$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186128842 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta$, which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nWe substitute\n\n$$\nu= \\dfrac{y^2}{x}\n$$\n\ninto the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x = y^{2} & ⟹ & \\frac{y^{2}}{x} = 1 & ⟹ & u = 1 \\\\ x = \\frac{y^{2}}{3} & ⟹ & \\frac{y^{2}}{x} = 3 & ⟹ & u = 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = \\frac{2}{x} & ⟹ & xy = 2 & ⟹ & v = 2 \\\\ y = \\frac{4}{x} & ⟹ & xy = 4 & ⟹ & v = 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\quad 2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v))/(∂(x, y)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3y^{2}}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{x}{3y^2} = -\\dfrac{1}{3u}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0\n$$\n\neverywhere inside $\\Delta$. In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta$.\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)dxdy &= ∬_(Δ)\\begin{vmatrix}(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) & dudv \\\\ = ∬_(Δ) & -\\frac{1}{3u}\\end{vmatrix}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}du\\int_{2}^{4}dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (\\ln 3 - \\ln 1) \\cdot (4 - 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\\ln 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186128842 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/e4/e4acf61532998ca5b0768b7edde16be779298c1be15ef11b14472678ba43043a.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/37/3706f0373bb39ebd41d78dd453896548ec03638a656e1fe36604f2a43108ba8e.png)\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u= \\dfrac{y^2}{x}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x={y}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitu