erse $A^+$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186161909 1071 "For the matrix \n![](images/d1/d19df62851eeb549464dd522cabcb4f5ab982ed4cdf39a81e6c18642299270f2.png) find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186161909 1074 "For an \"almost\" zero $m \\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n \\times m$ matrix with entries\n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{+} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}^{+} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6^{-1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4^{-1} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186161909 1074 "For an \"almost\" zero $m \\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n \\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{6}^{-1} & \\,\\,\\,0 & 0 \\\\ \\,\\,0 & \\,{4}^{-1} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186161910 1071 "![](images/95/95ef5c6139d13a561125764fc1658d03748004b4ee28843a7a3eb8dc7a37504a.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186161910 1071 "$\\