}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371844 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371851 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162076 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371575 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371789 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371796 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371803 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162096 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371768 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162096 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371775 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162096 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371782 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371816 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159296 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186162354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371762 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162354 1071 "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162354 1071 "$U_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, V_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = U_{r}Σ_{r}V_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A$, find the pseudoinverse $A^{+}$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186162354 1074 "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 16 & -22 \\\\ -22 & 16 & 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 8 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159362 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186162365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371810 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162365 1071 "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162365 1071 "$U_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ_{r} = [\\sqrt{10}], V_{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = U_{r}Σ_{r}V_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A$, find the pseudoinverse $A^{+}$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186162365 1074 "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160952 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186162615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373400 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162615 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} + 5x_{3}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2} - 4x_{1}x_{3} + 4x_{2}x_{3}$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n$y_{1}^{2} - y_{2}^{2} + y_{3}^{2}, {y_{1} = x_{1} - x_{2} - 2x_{3}; y_{2} = 0x_{2}; y_{3} = x_{3}$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186162615 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186162615 1074 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3})+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}))+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})^{2})+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373427 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373434 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371720 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371734 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373441 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373448 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372791 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372794 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372770 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372777 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186166965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372784 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186172094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371671 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186172139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371947 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186172139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371954 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186176402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174640 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186176402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371974 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186176402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186176402 1071 "![](images/33/3358dd177a848ea569fdeab6e39f4be268cc7325bb01c788e9bfe2ea2145d1eb.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 25$ for $y \\ge 0$, shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186176402 1071 "![](images/33/3358dd177a848ea569fdeab6e39f4be268cc7325bb01c788e9bfe2ea2145d1eb.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25$ for $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186176402 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/e5/e5371e711515feccdaab3f6b10734fb35bb59c724fc408c8b67b4b54132136ec.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186179432 1071 "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $\\psi(x,y) = e^{x} \\cos{y}$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186179432 1071 "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $\\psi(x,y) = e^{x} \\cos{y}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186179432 1074 "A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nif the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y},\\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $\\psi(x,y)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= \\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(e^{x}\\cos y) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\sin y, \\\\\nQ &= -\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e^{x}\\cos y) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\cos y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = e^{x} \\cos{y}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x, y) &= Pi + Qj \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\sin yi - e^{x}\\cos yj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186179432 1074 "A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y},\\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $\\psi(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $\\psi(x,y) = e^{x} \\cos{y}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186179433 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = x^4y\\,\\mathbf{i} - 2x^3y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\sin x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4x^2y\\,\\mathbf{i} -4x^2y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186179433 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = x^4y\\,\\mathbf{i} - 2x^3y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\sin x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4x^2y\\,\\mathbf{i} -4x^2y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186179433 1074 "A vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwith a simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 4x^{3}y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y}|=-4x^{3}y$.\nTherefore,\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 4x^3y + (-4x^3y) = 0$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = \\cos x\\sin y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y}|=-\\cos x\\sin y$.\nTherefore,\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\cos x \\sin y + (-\\cos x \\sin y) = 0$.\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 8xy, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y}|=-8x^{2}y$.\nTherefore,\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 8xy + (-8x^2y) \\neq 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186179433 1074 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4{x}^{3}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-4{x}^{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n= 4x^3y + (- 4x^3y)\n= 0\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n= \\cos x \\sin y + (- \\cos x \\sin y)\n= 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-8{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n= 8xy + (- 8x^2y)\n\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186179434 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+7 \\\\ u+v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overline{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overline{P'Q'}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overline{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $\\mathbf T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathb