5 true] [17592186180097 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ that passes through $P.$\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(-1,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is the $-1$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b803554d852d985c8feca61804a9e52423391fc4624a01c4199d0744fbf2e28d.png)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186188268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373201 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198024 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\ny=x, \\qquad\ny=7x, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $\\Delta$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T} : (u,v) \\to \\Big(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\: \\bbox[5pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\, \\Big),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198024 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n$y=x, \\qquad y=7x, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}$.\n\nA transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where\n\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\n\nmaps a rectangular region $\\Delta$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n$\\mathbf{T}: (u,v) \\to \\Big(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\: \\bbox[5pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\, \\Big)$,\nwhat could be the missing expression?" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198024 1074 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ &= {(u, v): a \\le u \\le b, c \\le v \\le d} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): a \\le u(x, y) \\le b, c \\le v(x, y) \\le d}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = a, \\qquad {\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = b, \\qquad {\\color{red}v(x,y)} = c, \\qquad {\\color{red}v(x,y)} = d\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D$.\n\nRewriting the equations\n\n$$\ny=x, \\qquad y=7x, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{x} = 1, \\frac{y}{x} = 7, xy^{2} = 3, xy^{2} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put\n\n$$\nu(x,y)={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nv(x,y)={\\color{red}xy^2}\n$$\n\nwe can define the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}: (x,y) \\to \\left(\\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}\\,, \\: \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{red}xy^2} \\right)\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{T}$, we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v$. Recall that we have\n\n$$\n{u = \\frac{y}{x}; v = xy^{2}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux$. Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nv = u^2x^3 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x^3 = \\dfrac{v}{u^2} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}\n$$\n\nback into $y=ux$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny =u\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}} = \\sqrt[3]{uv}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ can be given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: (u,v) \\to \\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\, \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\sqrt[3]{uv}} \\right)\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198024 1074 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = a, \\qquad \n{\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = b, \\qquad\n{\\color{red}v(x,y)} = c, \\qquad \n{\\color{red}v(x,y)} = d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\ny=x, \\qquad\ny=7x, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=1,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=7,\\,x{y}^{2}=3,\\,x{y}^{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}$ and $v(x,y)={\\color{red}xy^2},$ we can define the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1} : (x,y) \\to \\left( \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}\\,, \\: \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{red}xy^2} \\right).\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{T},$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u=\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ v=x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv = u^2x^3\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^3 = \\dfrac{v}{u^2} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}$ back into $y=ux,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny =u\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}} = \\sqrt[3]{uv}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ can be given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T} : (u,v) \\to \\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\, \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{ \\sqrt[3]{uv}} \\right).\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198051 1071 "![](images/f9/f9b1b286210d694cd3786fe38190216da503bfa3223114f86c8706f810313e33.png)\n\nThe curve $C_1$ has endpoints $A(5,3)$ and $B(1,3)$. Calculate the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\sqrt{y^3}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\,\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C_1$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1$, as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $\\mathbf F$ is source-free.*" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198051 1071 "![](images/f9/f9b1b286210d694cd3786fe38190216da503bfa3223114f86c8706f810313e33.png)\n\nThe curve $C_1$ has endpoints $A(5,3)$ and $B(1,3).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\sqrt{y^3}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n through $C_1$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector  $\\mathbf{n}_1,$ as shown above. \n\n*Hint: You're given that $\\mathbf F$ is source-free.*" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198051 1074 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$.\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$.\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{y^3}\\right) + \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\right) = 0+0 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, we know the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C_2$ is the straight line from $A(5,3)$ to $B(1,3)$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2a/2aeb26e58251923821d4058f886eada6a29b473ea70714b32e98c5338ddd0b28.png)\n\nNotice that both $\\mathbf n_1$ and $\\mathbf n_2$ are $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C_2$, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} P\\,\\textrm{d}y - Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b]$, and\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t)$.\n\nParametrizing $C_2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= 5i + 3j + t \\cdot [(i + 3j) - (5i + 3j)] \\\\\n&= (5 - 4t)i + 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1]$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= \\sqrt{y^{3}} = \\sqrt{(3)^{3}} = 3\\sqrt{3}, \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= \\sqrt{(5 - x)^{3}} = \\sqrt{(5 - (5 - 4t))^{3}} = 8\\sqrt{t^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(5 - 4t) =-4, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(3) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_(C_{2})F \\cdot n_{2}ds &= \\int_(C_{2})Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(3\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot (0) - (8\\sqrt{t^{3}}) \\cdot (-4)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}32t^{3/2}dt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{64t^{5/2}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= ((64(1)^{5/2})/(5)) - (((64(0)^{5/2})/(5)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1\\, \\textrm{d}s = \\dfrac{64}{5}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198051 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{y^3}\\right) + \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\right) = 0+0 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, we know the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_2$ is the straight line from $A(5,3)$ to $B(1,3)$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/2a/2aeb26e58251923821d4058f886eada6a29b473ea70714b32e98c5338ddd0b28.png)\n\nNotice that both $\\mathbf n_1$ and $\\mathbf n_2$ are $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C_2,$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{C_2} P\\,\\textrm{d}y - Q\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParametrizing $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\,i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(i+3\\,j)-(5\\,i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(5-4t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=3\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\sqrt{(5-x)^{3}}=\\sqrt{(5-(5-4t))^{3}}=8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-4t)=-4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (0)-(8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}})\\cdot (-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32{t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{64{t}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{64(1)^{5/2}}{5})-(\\frac{(64(0)^{5/2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1\\, \\textrm{d}s = \\dfrac{64}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198242 1071 "Consider the region $D$, given by\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy} \\leq 4, \\: 2x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere $u=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy$. Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198242 1071 "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy} \\leq 4, \\: 2x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy.$ Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198242 1074 "Notice that $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$.\n\nLet's find the image of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1}(D) &= {(u, v): 2 \\le \\sqrt{xy} \\le 4, 2x \\le y \\le 3x} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 2^{2} \\le (\\sqrt{xy})^{2} \\le 4^{2}, 2 \\le \\frac{y}{x} \\le 3} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 4 \\le xy \\le 16, 2 \\le \\frac{y}{x} \\le 3} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 4 \\le v \\le 16, 2 \\le u \\le 3} \\\\\n&= {(u, v): 2 \\le u \\le 3, 4 \\le v \\le 16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since\n\n$$\n2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy}\\leq 4\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nx \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nis well-defined." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198242 1074 "Notice that $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nLet's find the image of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le \\sqrt{xy}\\le 4,\\,2x\\le y\\le 3x} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,{2}^{2}\\le (\\sqrt{xy})^{2}\\le {4}^{2},\\,2\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 3} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le xy\\le 16,\\,2\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 3} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le v\\le 16,\\,2\\le u\\le 3} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,4\\le v\\le 16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy}\\leq 4,$ we must have $x \\neq 0.$ Therefore, $u=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ is well-defined." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198243 1071 "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve\n$(x+y)^2 + y = 3$ and the line $y=-1$.\nConsider the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v))$,\nwhere $u=x+y$ and $v=y$. Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198243 1071 "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$(x+y)^2 + y = 3$ and the line $y=-1.$\nConsider the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x+y$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198243 1074 "First, notice that we are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx+y = u\n$$\n\nand $y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x + y)^{2} + y = 3⟹u^{2} + v = 3⟹v = 3 - u^{2}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\ny =-1⟹v =-1\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{v = 3 - u^{2}; v =-1⟹{u = ± 2; v =-1\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, -1)$.\n\n![](images/84/84c624aab456b9a090c9c4e6d23c82d8e1f69425050b6f3ee9d4afd18e3f0cec.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D) = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\quad -1 \\leq v \\leq 3-u^2 \\big\\}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198243 1074 "First, notice that we are given that  \n\n![](images/61/61a3c6677ac0c20f5cccc6808fa7b078f91c3ad784d59167d11a9829cd0f86c3.png)\n\nSubstituting $x+y = u$ and $y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1\\,⟹\\,v=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=-1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 2 \\\\ v=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, -1).$\n\n![](images/84/84c624aab456b9a090c9c4e6d23c82d8e1f69425050b6f3ee9d4afd18e3f0cec.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\quad  -1 \\leq v \\leq 3-u^2 \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198248 1071 "Given the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{i} - 4x(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{j}$, which of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F= 0$\n2. $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field\n3. For two arbitrary curves $C_1$ and $C_2$, we have $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s \\neq \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198248 1071 "Given the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{i} - 4x(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{j},$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F= 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field\n\n- For two arbitrary curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ we have $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s \\neq \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198248 1074 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$.\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1,C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$.\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}$.\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain\n\n$$\nD=\\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2),\\qquad Q(x,y) =-4x(x^2+y^2)\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 8xy, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= -8xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 8xy + (-8xy) = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf F$ is source-free, we have that $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$ for all curves *with the same start and endpoints*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186198248 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1,C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D=\\mathbb{R}^2,$ where \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2),\\qquad Q(x,y) = - 4x(x^2+y^2).\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-8xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n=\n8xy + (-8xy) = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf F$ is source-free, we have that $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$ for all curves *with the same start and endpoints*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198368 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371449 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198368 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371456 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198369 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371463 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198369 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371470 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198369 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371477 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198369 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373304 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198369 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373380 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372096 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372103 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372110 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372117 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372124 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372061 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371926 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186198382