${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{tan}⁡y}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e32a019900b77c0bb84b4599b8899e3d0d32ae07d9674da126db34394b97ea24.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{tan}⁡y.$\n\n![](images/af/af629672ff2fd31eeae585ad4941fb8682941444cd34383392fb0a6bec24396e.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04dc917237ab002263e35fb4a84c264fef240fefaa456c5249d6e24d0c8ebe28.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b2/b2a6fc02441bb6013b6f54d49ceefafc1468b291ae4149847290135bbf2d2e56.png)\n\nWe now consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\n![](images/b9/b942dbfba8276421620df6258c9951c2b91a5d2dea18c84260c90b6cbe6f760b.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ then $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}^{\\pi /4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186199011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197906 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186199011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373298 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186199011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186199011 1071 "Consider the region $D$, given by\n$D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x + y \\le 2, 1 \\le x - y \\le 3}$\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere $u = x + y$ and $v = x - y + 1$. Find the representation of $T^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186199011 1071 "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,1\\le x-y\\le 3}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y+1.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186199011 1074 "Notice that ${T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nLet's find the image of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,1\\le x-y\\le 3} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,1+1\\le x-y+1\\le 3+1} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372732 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372738 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372744 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372750 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372759 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372607 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372627 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221448 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372817 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372797 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372800 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372803 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372808 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220249 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186221947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372621 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221947 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186221947 1071 "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt}|= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind $L{u(t - 1)\\cos (3πt)}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186221947 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-1)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-s}\\,L{g(t+1)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi (t+1)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi t+3\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the sum formula for cosine, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi t+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+1)} & =L{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-1)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-s}\\,L{g(t+1)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220821 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186222028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372768 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222028 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222028 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}+2x$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222028 1071 "$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπ}{2}x) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπ}{2}x)$\nLet $F(x) = 3x^{2} + 2x$ for $x ∈ [- 2, 2)$. The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_{0}$.$a_{0} =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186222028 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+2x$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({2}^{3}+{2}^{2})-((-2)^{3}+(-2)^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(12-(-4)) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220865 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186222035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372806 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222035 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222035 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=x$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222035 1071 "$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπ}{3}x) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπ}{3}x)$\nLet $F(x) = x$ for $x ∈ [- 3, 3)$. The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $a_{0}$.$a_{0} =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186222035 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=x$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220900 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186222040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372811 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222040 1071 "Find $L{u(t - 2)(t - 2)^{2}}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186222040 1071 "Find $L{u(t-2)(t-2)^{2}},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186222040 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-2)(t-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=2.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-2)=(t-2)^{2}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-2)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={t}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}:$\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{t}^{2}}=\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-2s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)(t-2)^{2}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229149 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372704 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229149 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372709 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229151 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372716 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372669 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372676 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372647 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372683 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372690 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372644 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372634 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372639 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228144 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186229664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228149 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186229664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228154 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186229664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372654 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372659 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372664 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229664 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix $Φ_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n$Φ_{0}^{′}(t) = AΦ_{0}(t), Φ_{0}(0) = I_{n}$.\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $Φ_{0}(t) = e^{At}$\n> $L2$: $x(t) = Φ_{0}(t)c$ for some constant vector $c$\n> $L3$: $x(0) = Φ_{0}(0)c$\n> $L4$: $Φ_{0}(0) = I_{n}$\n> $L5$: $x(0) = c$\n> $L6$: $x(t) = e^{At}x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from $L1$ by the\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L6$ follows from line $L2$ by substitution from lines {{field-3}}." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186229664 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$ \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by substitution from lines {{field-3}}." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186229664 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix, every solution of the system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Evaluating the identity $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ at $t=0$ gives \n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{evaluating both sides at }t=0.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Substituting ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ from $\\text{L1}$ and $c=x(0)$ from $\\text{L5}$ into $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and substitution from $\\text{L1}\\text{ and }\\text{L5}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231298 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny''+2y=f(x), \\qquad y'(0)=0,\\quad y'(1)=-5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p'(0) = 0$ and $y_p'(1) = 0.$*\n![](images/57/579150783e9ae1e69686ff319572bf5523934bf8eb266c5c19e9bf553aa8b0e1.png)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231298 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y''+2y=f(x), \\qquad y'(0)=0,\\quad y'(1)=-5$\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and even. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_p$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_p$ satisfies $y_p'(0) = 0$ and $y_p'(1) = 0$.*\n$\\frac{1}{xxx}?\\frac{1}{xxx}$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186231298 1074 "We write the solution as\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p\n$$\n\n, where $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y=0\n$$\n\nIts general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x)\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\ny'(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+y_p'(x)\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since $y_p'(0)=0$ and $y_p'(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny'(0)=y_c'(0)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(1)=y_c'(1)\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y'(0)=0{:}$\n$y^{′}(0)|= 0 + B\\sqrt{2}(1) + y_{p}^{′}(0); 0|= B\\sqrt{2} + 0; B|= 0$\nSo, $y_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)$.\n- Use $y'(1)=-5$ with $B=0{:}$\n$y^{′}(1)|=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}) + y_{p}^{′}(1); - 5|=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}) + 0; A|= (5)/(\\sqrt{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}))$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt gray]{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2})}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186231298 1074 "We write the solution as $y=y_c+y_p$, where $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation $y''+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\ny'(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)+y_p'(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since $y_p'(0)=0$ and $y_p'(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny'(0)=y_c'(0)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(1)=y_c'(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y'(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_c(x)=A\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use $y'(1)=-5$ with $B=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ -5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \\bbox[4pt, 