l equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(24I+250q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =24\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+250\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+62.5I=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186230370 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186231589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372841 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231589 1071 "The charge $q(t)$, in coulombs, and current $I(t)$, in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$\\frac{dI}{dt} + 5I + 6q = 12$.\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{9}{20}C$ and the initial current is $\\frac{9}{10}A$." 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186231589 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+6q=12.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{9}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $\\frac{9}{10}\\,\\text{A}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231589 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+6q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-3t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{9}{20}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{9}{10}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=12-5I-6q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{9}{10}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =12-5I(0)-6q(0) \\\\  & =12-5\\cdot \\frac{9}{10}-6\\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{9}{10}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ \\frac{9}{10} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{24}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{24}{5} & =-3A{e}^{-3(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ \\frac{24}{5} & =-3A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{24}{5}=-3A-2B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{33}{5}$ and $B=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{33}{5}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186230376 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186231590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372854 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231590 1071 "![](images/d3/d399a25c2a73883560aade5946007b21bf0b853e5d3ca262469df9b75e5843cd.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ in the circuit at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-5t/2}+B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{P}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{Q}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186231590 1071 "![](images/d3/d399a25c2a73883560aade5946007b21bf0b853e5d3ca262469df9b75e5843cd.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ in the circuit at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$I(t) = Ae^{-5t/2} + Be^{-t} + \\frac{P}{41}\\cos (2t) + \\frac{Q}{41}\\sin (2t)$,\nwhere $A$, $B$, $P$, and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P + Q$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533450 17592186045585 false] [17592186231590 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $14\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=14\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=14I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=14I+10q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(14I+10q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =14\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{5}{2}I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{5}{2}{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t/2}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\frac{7}{2}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\,\\, \\\\ +\\frac{5}{2}(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-\\frac{3}{2}\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-7\\alpha -\\frac{3}{2}\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}\\alpha +7\\beta =\\frac{5}{2}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -7\\alpha -\\frac{3}{2}\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{41}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{14}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{14}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t/2}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{14}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-3$ and $Q=14,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-3+14=11.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186371449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371450 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186371449 1069 "q-103899" 1319