6 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186371610 1071 "![](images/c2/c2de62e4bc1f48f627af8a045a54eecd25eba71786b6e1a29f493a2578b3f495.png)\n\nA graphic designer is reducing the diagonals of a simple square on the screen at a rate of $2.5\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate, in squared millimeters per second, is the area of the square decreasing when the length of the diagonal is $12.4\\,\\text{mm}?$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186371610 1072 #uuid "192dca39-9f7b-5c9d-8d03-bc591517190f" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186371610 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.5\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=12.4.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.5\\cdot 12.4=-31{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $31{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186371611 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371612 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186371611 1075 #edb/ref 17592186