071 "![](images/ce/ce36be89987c16762ad2b2e21d38cf78ec565f0aada219c982da6ea5eb958c1c.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{3z}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2x$ for $z\\ge 0,$ shown above." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186371967 1072 #uuid "6e23e7ac-d75a-538e-9787-5d9be89e54a3" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186371967 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(1,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/31/31a18ebb9608ff60a4a5ecbcfa8defcb6a64337b126cb5859cb042f1019b1194.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{3z}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\sqrt{({\\rho }^{2})^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{3z}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ[\\rho ]_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ-0)\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot [-{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot [{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 1759218