s}⁡y\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372096 1072 #uuid "3ae6735e-9449-5a99-b18f-9a251e2f61a2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372096 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372098 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372099 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372100 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372101 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372097 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372102 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372097 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372097 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372097 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372101 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372097 1080 #uuid "32807703-e305-5599-aa81-7f8c6300e6c9" 131941395334