69b32ba2e69fd9d795615399fe8e9e6330ff383e.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the curve shown above is $r=${{field-1}}." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372439 1072 #uuid "1caac910-a150-5a0e-853d-740a59df11e5" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372439 1074 "Notice that the given curve is a circle of radius $7$ centered at the origin.\n\nTherefore, its polar equation is $r=7.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372441 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372440 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372440 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372440 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372441 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372440 1080 #uuid "4e6a933a-2bf6-58e8-a869-858fde363dc9" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372441 1081 "7" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372441 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372441 1084 #uuid "d345aab8-8612-515f-939d-9141e8a1516c" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372443 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372442 1069 "q-276569" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372442 1071 "Compute the sum of the first $6$ terms of the geometric series $-4+1-\\frac{1}{4}+⋯.$\n\nExpress your answer as a fraction (do not round your answer).\n\n${S}_{6}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372442 1072 #uuid "d6a7ff04-c787-581e-bb85-fc042df42cc7" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372442 1074 "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nIn our case, ${a}_{1}=-4.$ To find the value of $r,$ we find the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{-4}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=-4,\\,N=6,$ and $r=-\\frac{1}{4}$ into our formula, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{6} & =-4\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{6})}{(1-(-\\frac{1}{4}))} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{6})}{(1+\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{6}-1}{{4}^{6}})}{(\\frac{5}{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{{4}^{6}-1}{{4}^{6}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot \\frac{4\\,095}{4\\,096} \\\\  & =-\\frac{819}{256}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372443 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372444 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372443 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372443 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372443 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372444 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372443 1080 #uuid "8df01a62-69e5-55eb-8b36-bbe7ede42a2c" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372444 1081 "-\\frac{819}{256}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372444 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372444 1084 #uuid "59b8008c-07f4-5a9a-8b02-82e149d37bb4" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372446 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372445 1069 "q-276574" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372445 1071 "Compute the sum of the first $9$ terms of the geometric series $2-10+50-⋯.$\n\n${S}_{9}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372445 1072 #uuid "7316413d-beb8-5f61-a294-030dab8c7935" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372445 1074 "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nIn our case, ${a}_{1}=5.$ To find the value of $r,$ we find the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-10}{2}=-5\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=2,\\,N=9,$ and $r=-5$ into our formula, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{9} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1-(-5)^{9}}{1-(-5)} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1+{5}^{9}}{1+5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1\\,953\\,126}{6} \\\\  & =651\\,042\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372446 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372447 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372446 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372446 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372446 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372447 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372446 1080 #uuid "344673b6-442d-533b-99e1-35940186210b" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372447 1081 "651,042" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372447 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372447 1084 #uuid "4757918c-6ca1-54c3-b5c6-4da3ed0a2134" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372449 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372448 1069 "q-276578" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372448 1071 "Find the missing factor in the sum of the first $14$ terms of the geometric series $7-35+175-⋯.$\n\nExpress your answer as a fraction (do not round your answer).\n\n${S}_{14}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (1-{5}^{14})$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372448 1072 #uuid "efa5953f-8f56-5a5b-9cf3-14ff95aebfaf" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372448 1074 "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nIn our case, ${a}_{1}=7.$ To find the value of $r,$ we find the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-35}{7}=-5\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=7,\\,N=14,$ and $r=-5$ into our formula, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{14} & =7\\cdot \\frac{1-(-5)^{14}}{1-(-5)} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1-{5}^{14}}{1+5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1-{5}^{14}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}\\cdot (1-{5}^{14})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372450 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372449 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372449 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372449 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372450 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372449 1080 #uuid "0a82c18a-0187-5a26-8013-c4b2e5a7abe2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372450 1081 "\\frac{7}{6}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372450 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372450 1084 #uuid "9ff6ad12-a3fc-5413-8737-80c73494fa5b" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372452 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372451 1069 "q-276703" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372451 1071 "Compute the sum of the series $262\\,144+32\\,768+⋯+1.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that $262\\,144={8}^{6}.$*\n\n$262\\,144+32\\,768+⋯+1=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372451 1072 #uuid "db86a36c-f776-56fa-b018-8ed415472ec3" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372451 1074 "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of this series, and $r$ is the common ratio. In our case, we have ${a}_{1}=262\\,144.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{32\\,768}{262\\,144}=\\frac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =262\\,144\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{262\\,144} & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{8}^{6}} & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{N-1} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{6} & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{N-1} \\\\ 6 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=262\\,144,$ $r=\\frac{1}{8},$ and $N=7$ in the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{7} & =262\\,144\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{7})}{(1-\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =262\\,144\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{{8}^{7}-1}{{8}^{7}})}{(\\frac{7}{8})} \\\\  & =262\\,144\\cdot \\frac{8}{7}\\cdot (\\frac{{8}^{7}-1}{{8}^{7}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,097\\,152}{7}\\cdot (\\frac{{8}^{7}-1}{{8}^{7}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,097\\,152}{7}\\cdot (\\frac{2\\,097\\,151}{2\\,097\\,152}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,097\\,151}{7} \\\\  & =299\\,593.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372452 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372453 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372452 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372452 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372452 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372453 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372452 1080 #uuid "cacaab6c-0e6a-5abf-bdac-0bc95b3ea54f" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372453 1081 "299,593" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372453 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372453 1084 #uuid "d7f09371-e74e-5338-9033-bb36b85b2504" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372455 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372454 1069 "q-276709" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372454 1071 "The first term of a geometric series is $10$, the last term is $\\frac{10}{81}$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{3}.$ What is the sum of this series?\n\nExpress your answer as a fraction (do not round your answer).\n\nThe sum of this series is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372454 1072 #uuid "58a14fd6-6c96-59d4-a0c6-ec4d699ff1aa" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372454 1074 "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio. In our case, we have ${a}_{1}=10$ and $r=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nWe must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{81} & =10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{81} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{3}^{4}} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{N-1} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{N-1} \\\\ 4 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=10,$ $r=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{5} & =10\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5})}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(\\frac{2}{3})}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{5}-1}{{3}^{5}} \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{242}{243} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,210}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372455 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372456 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372455 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372455 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372455 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372456 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372455 1080 #uuid "0309a20d-7e08-5043-846f-61875641dbcf" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372456 1081 "\\frac{1,210}{81}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372456 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372456 1084 #uuid "c40e6e6f-0422-5d81-9e51-34ece8184239" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372458 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372457 1069 "q-276714" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372457 1071 "The sixth term of the geometric series is $2\\,187$ and the seventh term is $6\\,561.$ What is the sum of the first $8$ terms?\n\n${S}_{8}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372457 1072 #uuid "a3eb7642-99ba-5598-bb23-03062562f02a" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372457 1074 "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{6}$ and ${a}_{7}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}}=\\frac{6\\,561}{2\\,187}=3\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of ${a}_{1}.$ To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 2\\,187 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 2\\,187 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot 243 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=9,$ $N=8,$ and $r=3$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{8} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1-{3}^{8}}{1-3} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1-{3}^{8}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot ({3}^{8}-1) \\\\  & =29\\,520.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372458 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372459 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372458 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372458 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372458 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372459 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372458 1080 #uuid "187e93ed-35c6-5e19-afb1-d3a5fb6711b5" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372459 1081 "29,520" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372459 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372459 1084 #uuid "757e79b2-12f9-5a81-a827-1a15db9b67ce" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372461 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372460 1069 "q-276719" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372460 1071 "The second term of a geometric series is $64$ and the third term is $16.$ What is the sum of the first $7$ terms?\n\nExpress your answer as a fraction (do not round your answer).\n\n${S}_{7}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372460 1072 #uuid "96aeb354-2d3f-5787-a4ed-fe24b636a2a2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372460 1074 "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio between the consecutive terms ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{16}{64}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of ${a}_{1}.$ To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2-1} \\\\ 64 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{1} \\\\ 64 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{64}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =256\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=256,\\,N=7,$ and $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{7} & =256\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =256\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{{1}^{7}}{{4}^{7}})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{(\\frac{3}{4})}\\cdot (\\frac{{4}^{7}-1}{{4}^{7}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,024}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{16\\,383}{16\\,384}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\,461}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372461 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372462 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372461 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372461 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372461 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372462 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372461 1080 #uuid "3cc916a4-7419-5ea6-9223-aa663482d99c" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372462 1081 "\\frac{5,461}{16}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372462 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372462 1084 #uuid "578b3f37-b5e7-5a09-822a-e77a119ef358" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372464 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372463 1069 "q-276731" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372463 1071 "The third term of a geometric series is $\\frac{7}{9}$ and the fourth term is $\\frac{7}{27}.$ What is the sum of the first $5$ terms?\n\nExpress your answer as a fraction (do not round your answer).\n\n${S}_{5}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372463 1072 #uuid "e33fba3d-d168-58f3-979d-7ae5f34441f5" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372463 1074 "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio between the consecutive terms ${a}_{3}$ and ${a}_{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{27})}{(\\frac{7}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of ${a}_{1}.$ To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{9} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=7,\\,N=5,$ and $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{5} & =7\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5})}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{{1}^{5}}{{3}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{(\\frac{2}{3})}\\cdot (\\frac{{3}^{5}-1}{{3}^{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{242}{243}) \\\\  & =\\frac{847}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372464 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372465 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372464 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372464 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372464 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372465 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372464 1080 #uuid "6033db7a-4d69-5568-b022-147d7c311af5" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372465 1081 "\\frac{847}{81}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372465 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372465 1084 #uuid "363712a7-4d95-5f01-9c2f-10ccf71195de" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372467 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372469 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372466 1069 "q-277198" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372466 1071 "The center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{4}=1$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372466 1072 #uuid "ea506366-210c-5fd2-83b0-b52d9cd95147" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372466 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=1$ and $k=-5,$ so the center is located at $(1,-5).$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372467 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372468 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372467 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372467 1077 "field-2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372467 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372468 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372467 1080 #uuid "1eee6fc9-216b-5f3c-bf2c-67ff272ef12f" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372468 1081 "-5" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372468 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372468 1084 #uuid "c6f8f8ef-20d2-5810-a5a5-d20ce3111b82" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372469 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372470 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372469 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372469 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372469 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372470 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372469 1080 #uuid "bcb83395-9403-5f77-b5d8-49ecf80f042e" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372470 1081 "1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372470 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372470 1084 #uuid "5df0b774-b279-5fca-bfd2-36244b70e24a" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372472 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372471 1069 "q-2774" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372471 1071 "![](images/46/4635202babbeec1f2308d624b5b3a8661f64a013e67b2df31f390da53ee17dcd.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are decreasing in length at a rate of $5\\text{ m/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square decreasing when the sides are $1\\text{ m}$ long?" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372471 1072 #uuid "4a3ddb6c-b587-5ebd-9ce4-5fb635e32a30" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372471 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{m/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-5.$ Also, when the sides are $1\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=1.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(-5)\\cdot 2(1)=-10{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square decreases at $10\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372472 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372473 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372472 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372474 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372472 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372475 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372472 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372476 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372472 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372477 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372472 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372472 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372472 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372475 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372472 1080 #uuid "6855b2f7-956f-5fd3-a4e4-08bd3d2ce035" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372473 1081 "18\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372473 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372473 1084 #uuid "cf58ccb3-1a0e-556d-afaa-da0b9af81525" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372474 1081 "12\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372474 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372474 1084 #uuid "48198598-3a47-5331-88f0-9fbe877d4390" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372475 1081 "10\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372475 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372475 1084 #uuid "808d892a-5bf4-51bf-adf9-e7ff4c1ae58c" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372476 1081 "4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372476 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372476 1084 #uuid "4653a187-5761-5618-97b4-e75267be95bb" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372477 1081 "2\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372477 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372477 1084 #uuid "929f3aa5-da20-5669-949a-ab47d66a4baf" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372479 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372481 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372483 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372478 1069 "q-277804" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372478 1071 "From **top to bottom**, the points where the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=16$ intersects the line $x=5$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372478 1072 #uuid "de41e156-2c34-5849-b191-321ad9f6dfc9" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372478 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=5,$ we substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (5)^{2}-{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 25-{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =16-25 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ y & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=5$  at the points $(5,3)$ and $(5,-3).$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372479 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372480 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372479 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372479 1077 "field-2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372479 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372480 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372479 1080 #uuid "b0604a26-4e06-5b10-a21f-dcbd6a79de03" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372480 1081 "3" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372480 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372480 1084 #uuid "245b5569-84b0-5d62-b305-9438e4ff8cd8" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372481 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372482 13194139