 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372627 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372627 1072 #uuid "1d3c52b7-9c42-5f5e-9301-6c966cfe0aa8" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372627 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(7\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t+28\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡28\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡28\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372628 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372629 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372628 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372630 13194139533450 175921860