o find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+21(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(21\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-21\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}21\\alpha +21\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -21\\alpha +21\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{42},\\beta =\\frac{5}{42}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{42}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{5}{42}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(\\frac{5}{42})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{5}{42})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372640 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372641 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372640 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372640 1077 "field-1" 13194139533