e right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(\\beta -2\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha -2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\beta -2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha -2\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{6}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372645 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372646 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372645 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372645 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372645 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372646 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372645 1080 #uuid "ad04f2e2-3540-54e1-be2e-99fade1d5cbc" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372646 1081 "\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}(x)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}(x)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372646 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372646 1084 #uuid "cb23e6da-438d-5d69-b693-36217361c2a5" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372648 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372647 1069 "q-333551" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372647 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =1,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.50.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372647 1072 #uuid "b6ea5569-1b38-5840-af43-7864b3cc3a00" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372647 1074 "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.50.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372648 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372649 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372648 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372650 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372648 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372651 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372648 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372652 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372648 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372653 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372648 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372648 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372648 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372653 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372648 1080 #uuid "8a5c4cbe-6fc4-557c-90ae-7db2c270cd63" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372649 1