ical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.8,$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.1,$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3,$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is **unstable** for the values $\\lambda Δx=2.1,$ and $\\lambda Δx=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only\"." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372677 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372678 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372677 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372679 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372677 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372680 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372677 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372681 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372677 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372682 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372677 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372677 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372677 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372679 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372677 1080 #uuid "d82b587d-88f5-5209-8711-b68207cf6390" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372678 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372678 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372678 1084 #uuid "caa6f4b3-cc01-5d85-9a4d-de489c723ea6" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372679 1081 "III and IV only" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372679 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372679 1084 #uuid "fab3b6cc-ea3a-5b82-9f35-99b19503a6c0" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372680 1081 "III only" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372680 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372680 1084 #uuid "d30e912d-cf3c-5dbf-bdb8-8e8ef96ec53c" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372681 1081 "IV only" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372681 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372681 1084 #uuid "7e067211-3d0e-5029-ad94-c332dfda461d" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372682 1081 "I, II, III, and IV" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372682 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372682 1084 #uuid "c7831ff0-a604-5c04-b30b-fe924ff73379" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372684 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372683 1069 "q-334425" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372683 1071 "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.3$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.8$\n- $\\lambda Δx=3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372683 1072 #uuid "dd6bd92c-8765-5d3c-9d52-912010fd3181" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372683 1074 "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.3)| & =|1-0.3+\\frac{(0.3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{59\\,267}{80\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.740…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.4)| & =|1-2.4+\\frac{(2.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{349}{625}| \\\\  & =0.558…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.8)| & =|1-2.8+\\frac{(2.8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.8)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.8)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{639}{625}| \\\\  & =1.0224>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3+\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11}{8}| \\\\  & =1.375<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [