{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y({e}^{x}+1)+{y}_{\\text{new}}({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,y({e}^{x}+1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}\\,y({e}^{x}+1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,y({e}^{x}+5)+\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,y({e}^{x}+5) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3-{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,y({e}^{x}+5) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y({e}^{x}+5)}{3-{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y({e}^{x}+5)}{3-{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({e}^{0}+5)}{3-{e}^{1/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3-{e}^{1/2}} \\\\  & ≈4.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈4.44.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372745 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372746 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372745 1075 #edb/ref