\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.2\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2.2.\n$$\n\nWhen the diagonal length is $h=10,$ substituting into the expression above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=(-2.2)(10)=-22{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square opening is decreasing at a rate of $22{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372912 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372913 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372912 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372912 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372912 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372913 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372912 1080 #uuid "240a125a-7965-503b-98eb-0c6543b7dbff" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372913 1081 "22" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372913 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372913 1084 #uuid "a1297eee-ae31-559a-8063-29c28074e2db" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372915 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372914 1069 "q-344652" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372914 1071 "![](images/0f/0ff48bba31ebfe609c1786e3bdcd540aba28b125e67daaac17ff2aed3301749c.png)\n\nA robotics system is expanding a square inspection window by increasing the lengths of its diagonals at a rate of $2.4\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the window increasing when each diagonal measures $10\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372914 1072 #uuid "9ad324ac-7c69-5324-bd8a-391926d00de5" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372914 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=2.4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=10.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2.4\\cdot 10=24{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square inspection window is increasing at a rate of \n$24{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372915 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372916 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372915 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372915 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372915 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372916 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372915 1080 #uuid "89dd717b-79a4-558f-b04b-2b3c3b396d56" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372916 1081 "24" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372916 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372916 1084 #uuid "edc138bc-fe8e-58e0-9d34-afbb1a5f1520" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372918 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372917 1069 "q-345149" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372917 1071 "![](images/48/4852aaf3945331346a68fe3f967658665f71d81be0e28fd0d34fd965c7045596.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372917 1072 #uuid "9d96ca73-b13f-58db-8ae6-317919c23853" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372917 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=10I+200q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+200q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372918 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372919 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372918 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372920 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372918 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372921 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372918 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372922 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372918 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372923 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372918 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372918 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372918 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372919 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372918 1080 #uuid "3b5f9ae5-8d9d-5c4b-9f0c-42d42f19c0c3" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372919 1081 "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372919 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372919 1084 #uuid "bf500311-ba0c-5251-a16d-ae6ce700c2e4" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372920 1081 "2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200I=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372920 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372920 1084 #uuid "198292fb-0c70-5ea9-acb2-c7ed4d73d67f" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372921 1081 "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372921 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372921 1084 #uuid "17ba4a0e-ed7f-54bf-adfb-a5cffd850e8e" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372922 1081 "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+100I=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372922 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372922 1084 #uuid "5b203598-dfae-5eba-a462-ef03871d8a31" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372923 1081 "\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200I=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372923 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372923 1084 #uuid "de2d56c9-274b-5ce4-a18e-6325e1ac2464" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372925 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372924 1069 "q-345229" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372924 1071 "![](images/7a/7abc70a954ec2bb29c909f92b5a88655f8f3c66270ca0947487a8bc5f5e4deda.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372924 1072 #uuid "0fe4da9b-3724-51c1-9459-24000ff26a62" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372924 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $24\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{\\frac{1}{8}}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n24=12I+8q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(24) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12I+8q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+2I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=6-3I-2q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=2$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =6-3I(0)-2q(0) \\\\  & =6-3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=2$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{-0}+B{e}^{-0} \\\\ 2 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =-A{e}^{-0}-2B{e}^{-0} \\\\ -1 & =-A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=A+B \\\\ -1=-A-2B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=3$ and $B=-1.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=3{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372926 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372927 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372928 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372929 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372930 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372925 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372925 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372925 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372928 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372925 1080 #uuid "1b2a0949-4487-5bb2-8e80-6a5b82332303" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372926 1081 "I(t)=2{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372926 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372926 1084 #uuid "b81dfb34-9217-5a69-8561-4b65c7914497" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372927 1081 "I(t)=3{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t}" 13