lectromotive force (EMF) of $3\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{\\frac{1}{3}}=3q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n3=5I+3q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(3) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(5I+3q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{3}{2}I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-3t/2}$ and $I={e}^{-t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-3t/2}+B{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{3}{2}q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}I(0)-\\frac{3}{2}q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 1-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-3(0)/2}+B{e}^{-0} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3t/2}-B{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3(0)/2}-B{e}^{-0} \\\\ -2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ -2=-\\frac{3}{2}A-B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=2$ and $B=-1.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=2{e}^{-3t/2}-{e}^{-t}.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372933 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372934 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372935 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372936 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372937 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372932 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372932 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372932 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372935 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372932 1080 #uuid "c7592773-6af2-5116-b635-86d74e652343" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [1759