 voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372938 1072 #uuid "c9acdec8-78fa-5121-85f5-7da3b379926b" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372938 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ff2c0f79fcacd14a8243aceac56beceee0d3c585a7c7e904d0d38dc1e6da62ce.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.5}=2q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n12=9I+2q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(12) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(9I+2q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{1}{2}I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-2t}$ and $I={e}^{-t/4}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t/4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $1\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=3-\\frac{9}{4}I-\\frac{1}{2}q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=2$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =3-\\frac{9}{4}I(0)-\\frac{1}{2}q(0) \\\\  & =3-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=2$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{-2(0)}+B{e}^{-0/4} \\\\ 2 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}B{e}^{-t/4}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =-2A{e}^{-2(0)}-\\frac{1}{4}B{e}^{-0/4} \\\\ -2 & =-2A-\\frac{1}{4}B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=A+B \\\\ -2=-2A-\\frac{1}{4}B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{6}{7}$ and $B=\\frac{8}{7}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{6}{7}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{8}{7}{e}^{-t/4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $0.4$ seconds, in amperes, is\n\n$$\nI(0.4)=\\frac{6}{7}{e}^{-2(0.4)}+\\frac{8}{7}{e}^{-(0.4)/4}≈1.419,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372939 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372940 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372939 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372941 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372939 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372942 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372939 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372943 13194139533450 17592186045585 tr