id "e7be00b9-6c55-5cfa-b2d6-fc29b1968d21" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372980 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $2\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=2\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=2I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{\\frac{1}{7}}=7q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=2I+7q+\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(2I+7q+\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+7\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+7I=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+7{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +4\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -4\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{5}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t))+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t))$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}}=2.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372982 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372983 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372984 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372985 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372981 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372986 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372981 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372981 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372981 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372985 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372981 1080 #uuid "5db51297-73de-5c50-b0d9-e1bb397c5046" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372982 1081 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372982 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372982 1084 #uuid "e65a3cda-f720-5cdb-954d-d23d1a593fa9" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372983 1081 "\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372983 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372983 1084 #uuid "dfe8fdad-608f-55fc-a07c-252211513843" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372984 1081 "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372984 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372984 1084 #uuid "93dff8b7-d1db-5dce-800c-2cdf1413a3b9" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372985 1081 "2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372985 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372985 1084 #uuid "19625b95-cac9-5c06-b172-c87d063b6572" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372986 1081 "\\frac{8}{5}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372986 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372986 1084 #uuid "8251ad5e-9cea-599c-92df-e746540c114b" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372988 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372987 1069 "q-346461" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372987 1071 "The table below gives the carbon dioxide emission rate $r(t)$, in tons per day, from a factory.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(days)}$ | $0$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,\\text{(tons/day)}$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,6]$ with two subintervals to approximate the total carbon dioxide emitted (in tons).\n\nThe total carbon dioxide emitted is\n{{field-1}} tons." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372987 1072 #uuid "0861b040-016e-5043-83cb-0a5fe8f87b3b" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372987 1074 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=r(t)$ over $[0,6].$ In this case, we have $n=2$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is computed as \n\n$$\nA≈r(2)Δ{t}_{1}+r(6)Δ{t}_{2},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $r(t)$ | $-$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total accumulation of emissions. So, the total carbon dioxide emitted is $8$ tons." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372988 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372989 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372988 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372988 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372988 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372989 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372988 1080 #uuid "98c4b2e0-2772-5263-99b8-1a14a7df43f2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372989 1081 "8" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372989 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372989 1084 #uuid "6826a19d-79af-57ff-afac-cff71e445c93" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372991 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372990 1069 "q-348675" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372990 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),\\,t-1⟩,$ what is $|r(2)|?$\n\n$|r(2)|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372990 1072 #uuid "5e394120-3869-5269-8095-84ba7453c5cb" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372990 1074 "Evaluating the function at $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (2)}{2}),\\,2-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(2)| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372991 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372992 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372991 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372991 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372991 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372992 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372991 1080 #uuid "6117625f-05ef-53e9-943b-6f3c65d34905" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372992 1081 "1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372992 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372992 1084 #uuid "a0b2fd90-b2d0-5bff-89b9-c0cfe2e250f9" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372994 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372996 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372993 1069 "q-351170" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372993 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{6}{t},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$a(3)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372993 1072 #uuid "57f4ce54-f786-5478-bb5f-6c5aa29043f7" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372993 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{6}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}},\\,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=3,$ we substitute $t=3$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(3) & =⟨-\\frac{6}{(3)^{2}},\\,6(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{6}{9},\\,18⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{2}{3},\\,18⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372994 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372995 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372994 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372994 1077 "field-2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372994 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372995 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372994 1080 #uuid "3575c776-a636-5af4-bb6c-bf1b48c7fb08" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372995 1081 "18" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372995 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372995 1084 #uuid "a52f72cb-965a-5902-9b77-1c6820914957" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372996 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372997 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372996 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372996 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372996 1079 #edb/ref 17592186372997 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372996 1080 #uuid "6feedaa5-2210-528d-8a55-7c8436080bf4" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372997 1081 "-\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372997 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372997 1084 #uuid "9601062b-863e-5c77-bb21-d5c187ee3108" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372999 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373001 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372998 1069 "q-351386" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372998 1071 "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{e}^{3t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372998 1072 #uuid "32fc8a7f-9ca9-5c2b-8df3-77d04cc9f50d" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372998 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,3{e}^{3t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{3t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,9{e}^{3t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373000 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372999 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372999 1077 "field-2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372999 1079 #edb/ref 17592186373000 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186372999 1080 #uuid "1b329b57-06f0-5791-b973-f62012577b79" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373000 1081 "9{e}^{3t}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373000 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373000 1084 #uuid "6efc21aa-4c58-5ab6-9da1-0d281d866c48" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373001 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373002 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373001 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373001 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373001 1079 #edb/ref 17592186373002 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373001 1080 #uuid "181fecab-807b-598e-b988-9a54158ef4cf" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373002 1081 "-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373002 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373002 1084 #uuid "9d65d9fb-3780-5cdb-9079-4cbb6ff8bfae" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373004 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373003 1069 "q-352720" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373003 1071 "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨(t-4)^{3},\\,(t-4)^{2}{e}^{-2{t}^{2}+3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373003 1072 #uuid "0ff326db-e280-5213-a440-a9970c126c6d" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373003 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-4)^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-4)^{2}{e}^{-2{t}^{2}+3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(t-4)^{2},\\,2(t-4){e}^{-2{t}^{2}+3}-4t(t-4)^{2}{e}^{-2{t}^{2}+3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(t-4)^{2},\\,(t-4)(2-4t(t-4)){e}^{-2{t}^{2}+3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3(t-4)^{2},\\,(t-4)(2-4t(t-4)){e}^{-2{t}^{2}+3}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t-4)^{2}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=4, \\\\ (t-4)(2-4t(t-4)){e}^{-2{t}^{2}+3}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=4,\\text{ }2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=4$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373004 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373005 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373004 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373004 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373004 1079 #edb/ref 17592186373005 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373004 1080 #uuid "dd222771-aa78-5640-aa10-c45d9383bdd6" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373005 1081 "4" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373005 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373005 1084 #uuid "7f0adb04-a9ed-5f76-b35d-2f262185bb0a" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373007 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373006 1069 "q-353406" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373006 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=2y+1$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373006 1072 #uuid "a7af07c0-9b83-53a4-a5ca-c1038d314a70" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373006 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y+1)=2\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373007 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373008 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373007 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373007 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373007 1079 #edb/ref 17592186373008 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373007 1080 #uuid "efb67ce8-e9cf-5cc6-9526-1e17caa84c54" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373008 1081 "\\pi \\sqrt{5}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373008 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373008 1084 #uuid "aa604d08-0716-553b-a8e9-f80a8988f32c" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373010 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373009 1069 "q-355560" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373009 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}$ that satisfies $y(2)=-1.$\n\n|  | $y(x)=$ | $x$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373009 1072 #uuid "c1491d28-f486-5d5a-8bd7-310f4893ab88" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373009 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =(4{x}^{-2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (4{x}^{-2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -{y}^{-1} & =-4{x}^{-1}+x+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =-\\frac{4}{x}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x}-x-C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{4-{x}^{2}-Cx}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{4-{x}^{2}-Cx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(2)=-1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4-{2}^{2}-C(2)} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{-2C} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{C} & =-1 \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{x}{4-x-{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373010 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373011 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373010 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373010 1077 "field-1" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373010 1079 #edb/ref 17592186373011 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373010 1080 #uuid "c01b75b0-e7f0-5237-8118-7d26642ee499" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373011 1081 "4-x-{x}^{2}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373011 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373011 1084 #uuid "cad08fd0-a60c-5289-bfae-917d24b4cf51" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373013 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373012 1069 "q-36351" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373012 1071 "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)$ at the point $(1,-1,0).$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373012 1072 #uuid "a1e0fe3a-1bd3-5ee4-ab1a-74e46b29c2c8" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373012 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z$, then our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,0).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y)-z)k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2x+y}\\text{ }j-1\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+(-1)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2(1)+(-1)}\\text{ }j-k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2}=y+1=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373013 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373014 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373013 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373015 13194139533450 1759