/s}.$ At what rate are the lengths of the sides of the square decreasing when the sides are $10\\text{ m}$ long?" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373069 1072 #uuid "a12f02aa-618c-514d-801d-85da1570bd91" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373069 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/d4/d410da34cd3c90570266b32351b41560f804f8c04be77e9089414a5cf374909b.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-40\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-40 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{20}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{20}{10}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lengths of the sides are decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373071 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373072 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373073 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373070 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373074 13194139533450 1759218604