592186045585 true] [17592186373324 1072 #uuid "6d3957bc-82e8-5ac2-b7a9-4a6d13bcdf98" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373324 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-4)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373325 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373326 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373325 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373327 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373325 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373328 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373325 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373329 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373325 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373330 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373325 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373325 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373325 1079 #edb/ref 17592186373327 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373325 1080 #uuid "14b44c30-bfb7-5726-84f7-243f272a60e0" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373326 1081 "2\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373326 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373326 1084 #uuid "f6feecd4-8822-5f34-9d43-bd14c718bbd8" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373327 1081 "\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373327 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373327 1084 #uuid "17148ef3-6acc-5564-b61d-01018de840fb" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373328 1081 "\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373328 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373328 1084 #uuid "4153ac73-6a9e-55d0-85c9-e7e7af03259b" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373329 1081 "2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373329 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373329 1084 #uuid "f2b0c395-4679-520f-9797-456665f24d41" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373330 1081 "2\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373330 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373330 1084 #uuid "94ce7d37-7650-556c-afec-2c465c9a5502" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373332 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373331 1069 "q-83491" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373331 1071 "![](images/e0/e0331b5d4c9fa3c823d6596c1ab147b8ee459feb4a4feaed3c614e2639ae77e5.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=4x-xy$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}},$ shown above." 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373331 1072 #uuid "f539d9c8-b40f-5e02-8e74-4380e9158356" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373331 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-y,\\,-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4-y=0 \\\\ -x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,4).$ Since the point $(0,4)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(0) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,4-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=0,$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(0,4).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,4),\\,(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $8$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $8$ at the points $(2,0).$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373333 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373334 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373335 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373336 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373337 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373332 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373332 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373332 1079 #edb/ref 17592186373336 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373332 1080 #uuid "3a0d98f6-add4-58fe-abc8-484ea418fdb2" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373333 1081 "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373333 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373333 1084 #uuid "06940bef-a215-5420-8df8-1b3ac15f920f" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373334 1081 "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373334 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373334 1084 #uuid "ca155e95-50cc-5275-acc6-fa54f8093bdc" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373335 1081 "${f}_{min}=4$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373335 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373335 1084 #uuid "662e6c67-053c-5fa1-acb1-c6fef45dcb53" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373336 1081 "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373336 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373336 1084 #uuid "bcde84a3-5a11-524f-8852-258641bb7adf" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373337 1081 "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=-4$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373337 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373337 1084 #uuid "048eff16-8812-5b00-a086-bac6d33306cf" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373339 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373338 1069 "q-83943" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373338 1071 "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-2y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $y-(x-2y)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=3.$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373338 1072 #uuid "851a883c-83e2-57d3-b805-41682b65a2ea" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373338 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-2y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-2y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(x-2y)^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v-{u}^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}+2 \\\\ v=3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,3).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{2}+2\\le v\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca755cd11609e7c704adb37eb867ff8cc48bc5fc44b29cd777269be5791d9160.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3-({u}^{2}+2))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373340 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373341 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373342 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373343 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373344 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373339 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373339 1077 "selection" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373339 1079 #edb/ref 17592186373343 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373339 1080 #uuid "d0492151-0ea1-564c-95ed-c1870ce912b6" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373340 1081 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533450 17592186045585 true] [17592186373340 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533450 1759218604